    "available_at": "2026-11-11T17:39:59.729471+00:00",
    "body": "EICMA 2026 • MTPeak Stole Some of the Spotlight at EICMA 2026 With Insane Power and Retro Looks on Three Wheels\nBMW Adds South Korea's Samsung SDI and LG Energy Solution to Gen6 High-Voltage Battery Supply Chain\n12 photos\nPhoto: BMW\nBMW has recently confirmed that Samsung SDI and LG Energy Solution will become integral parts of the Munich-based automaker's electric vehicle supply chain. The South Korean companies will supply cells for sixth-generation batteries from regional facilities, beginning in the latter part of the 2020s.\nThe question is, why? Doesn't BMW have sufficient high-voltage battery cell suppliers to satisfy demand? The move is a little more complex, though. Simply put, BMW has expanded its battery cell supplier portfolio while bringing a new layer of regional sourcing to its growing electric vehicle production network.\nBy contracting the folks at Samsung and LG, the German automaker isn't abandoning existing partners or Chinese suppliers. Instead, the manufacturer wants more options in the regions where it builds electric vehicles. This way, it reduces exposure to individual supplier-level issues and long-distance logistics.\nBMW will continue sourcing Euro-produced cells from Chinese heavyweights CATL and EVE Energy, with Samsung SDI joining that supplier group for good measure. LG Energy Solution, meanwhile, will become another supplier for the automaker's North American operations. BMW even has a name for this particular strategy, calling it \"local for local\" in the attached media release.\nIn addition to selecting a battery supplier with a factory somewhere on the same continent, the L4L philosophy also means that battery cells will travel shorter distances between their production facilities, the plants where they morph into high-voltage battery assemblies, and vehicle assembly plants. An efficiently coordinated manufacturing network, if you will.\nPhoto: BMW The agreements with Samsung and LG concern Gen6 battery cells, which define the Neue Klasse-based i3 and iX3 lines. Gen6 technologies have also carried over to Cluster Architecture-based electric models . Samsung SDI will supply these large-format cylindrical cells, which are completely different in design from the fifth-generation rectangular prismatic cells, to European facilities.\nLG, on the other hand, will support North American operations. The Spartanburg facility in the United States of America comes to mind, while BMW Group Plant San Luis Potosi in Mexico prepares for volume production of electric vehicles in 2027. The Mexican plant has received 800 million euros to prepare for Neue Klasse vehicle production, which includes the iX3 and i3.\nChinese battery cell suppliers remain important for BMW, yet choosing Samsung and LG for Europe and North America further demonstrates that BMW is reducing its dependence on CATL and EVE. It's only natural to do so, considering the Asian country's dominant position in cell manufacturing and the processing of battery materials.\nThink about the consequences of battery diplomacy for Europe and North America for a minute. Not even political leaders are okay with China's dominance in this domain, let alone car manufacturers that are mostly dependent on Chinese battery cell suppliers. Looking at the bigger picture, what BMW did here was to reduce the risk that a disruption, be it a geopolitical situation or simply an individual supplier-level problem, will interfere with electric vehicle production.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nBMW Samsung LG battery EV\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT Ultra-Revolutionary UHV 100 Heralds a New Era of American Yacht Design",
